    <body>
      <sec id="article-140076.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada&#x000a0;syndrome constitutes a multisystem autoimmune disorder characterized by granulomatous inflammation targeting melanocyte-containing tissues, including the uveal tract, meninges, inner ear, and skin. The condition is a significant cause of noninfectious uveitis worldwide, although the incidence in the US remains low. Pathogenesis is attributed to T cell&#x02013;mediated immune responses against melanocytic antigens in genetically susceptible individuals, with increased prevalence among patients of Asian, Hispanic, Middle Eastern, and Native American ancestry.</p>
        <p>Clinical manifestations typically evolve through prodromal, acute uveitic, and convalescent phases, with bilateral panuveitis, serous retinal detachments, headache, meningismus, tinnitus, dysacusis, vitiligo, poliosis, and alopecia. Diagnosis is clinical and supported by ocular imaging and cerebrospinal fluid analysis when indicated. Management requires prompt initiation of high-dose systemic corticosteroids followed by immunomodulatory therapy to prevent recurrence. Delayed or inadequate treatment elevates the risk of chronic recurrent inflammation, cataract, glaucoma, subretinal fibrosis, and permanent visual impairment. Prognosis depends on early recognition and sustained immunosuppression.</p>
        <p>This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance learners' competence in evaluating and managing&#x000a0;Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada syndrome. Participants will advance their mastery of the condition's etiology, risk factors, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and&#x000a0;best diagnostic and therapeutic practices. Improved skills will equip clinicians to collaborate with interprofessional teams caring for affected individuals.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the clinical and diagnostic features indicative of Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada syndrome.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply best practices in the management of&#x000a0;Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada&#x000a0;syndrome,&#x000a0;emphasizing prompt initiation of systemic corticosteroids and immunomodulatory therapy to mitigate complications.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Improve patient understanding of Vogt&#x02013;Koyanagi&#x02013;Harada&#x000a0;syndrome, including disease progression, treatment rationale, and lifestyle strategies.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with the interprofessional team to educate, treat, and monitor individuals with Vogt&#x02013;Koyanagi&#x02013;Harada&#x000a0;syndrome to enhance their quality of life and overall health outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=140076&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=140076">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Vogt&#x02013;Koyanagi&#x02013;Harada (VKH)&#x000a0;syndrome is a multisystem disorder affecting vision and hearing. Historical descriptions date to the 12th century, but the eponym derives from 3 20th-century physicians who characterized the condition's manifestations. Alfred Vogt initially reported bilateral iridocyclitis and eyebrow depigmentation in 1906. Yoshizo Koyanagi later described bilateral serous retinal detachments associated with cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) pleocytosis in 1929. Einosuke Harada described acute diffuse choroiditis as a hallmark feature of the disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r1">[1]</xref> VKH manifests due to loss of immune tolerance to melanocytes within the meninges, eyes, skin, hair, and ears.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>VKH&#x000a0;syndrome exhibits a distinct phenotypic profile across early and late stages. Although clinical findings are identifiable, the underlying pathophysiologic mechanisms remain incompletely understood. Recent technological advancements have clarified several aspects of VKH&#x000a0;syndrome, including genetic predisposition and immunologic mechanisms. Ophthalmic complications are frequent, and prompt recognition combined with early therapeutic intervention reduces morbidity and improves long-term visual outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>VKH&#x000a0;syndrome is primarily managed with medical therapy, although surgical intervention may be required for complications, such as subretinal fibrosis, glaucoma, or cataracts. Prognosis depends largely on the stage at which treatment is initiated, ranging from quiescent disease with minimal symptoms to severe, vision-threatening outcomes and chronic pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r5">[5]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>VKH&#x000a0;syndrome is classically considered a spectrum of disorders.&#x000a0;Vogt&#x02013;Koyanagi syndrome represents&#x000a0;the anterior presentation, characterized by severe anterior uveitis and extraocular manifestations, including vitiligo, alopecia, poliosis, and dysacusis. Harada disease comprises the severe posterior presentation, including posterior uveitis with diffuse choroiditis and meningeal involvement, often accompanied by&#x000a0;CSF abnormalities. The term "Vogt&#x02013;Koyanagi&#x02013;Harada syndrome" encompasses the complete disorder, combining both anterior and posterior features.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>The precise etiology of VKH&#x000a0;syndrome remains incompletely defined.&#x000a0;Current evidence suggests the development of T cell&#x02013;mediated immunity (type IV hypersensitivity) targeting melanocytes following exposure to an inciting viral or environmental factor.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r7">[7]</xref> Cytomegalovirus and Epstein&#x02013;Barr virus have been implicated, although no definitive causal relationship has been established.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r9">[9]</xref> The disease results from loss of immune tolerance to melanocytes, leading to nonnecrotizing granulomatous inflammation in ocular, auditory, cutaneous, and hair tissues.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>VKH&#x000a0;syndrome is strongly associated with specific human leukocyte antigen (HLA) class II genes, particularly HLA-DR4, and the allelic variants HLA-DRB10405 and HLA-DRB10410.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r12">[12]</xref> HLA-DQ4 represents a significant risk factor, especially in Japanese populations, frequently occurring in linkage disequilibrium with HLA-DR4 alleles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r13">[13]</xref> A meta-analysis confirmed the association between VKH and HLA-DR4/DRB104, noting variability in the strength of this relationship across ethnic groups. HLA-DRB10404, *0405, and *0410 were identified as risk suballeles, whereas *0401 confers relative protection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>Non-HLA genes regulating lymphocyte activity and production of interleukins (ILs) 12 and 17 also play critical roles in the pathogenesis of VKH&#x000a0;syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r15">[15]</xref> Additional contributing factors include downregulation of microRNAs affecting IL production and alterations in noncoding RNAs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r17">[17]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Regional differences exist in the prevalence of VKH&#x000a0;syndrome among patients presenting with uveitis. VKH&#x000a0;syndrome represents a common cause of panuveitis in Asian countries and the Middle East, including India, Japan, Thailand, and Saudi Arabia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r19">[19]</xref> The condition is relatively rare in the US, accounting for only 3% to 4% of referrals to tertiary care centers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r20">[20]</xref> Approximately 800 new cases are reported annually in Japan.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r21">[21]</xref> Population-based Japanese data historically indicated an incidence of approximately 6.5 per million per year and a prevalence of 15.5 per million.&#x000a0;Estimates from Western populations are lower, with 1.5 to 6 cases per million per year reported in some US series.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r22">[22]</xref></p>
        <p>Hispanic and Native American predominance has been noted in North American cases of VKH syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r23">[23]</xref> Countries in South America with substantial native and Hispanic populations, such as Colombia, have not demonstrated a significant increase in VKH&#x000a0;syndrome's incidence compared with the US.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r25">[25]</xref> Although VKH is traditionally associated with populations with higher skin pigmentation, a study in a predominantly White population identified a higher proportion of White non-Hispanic patients, emphasizing the importance of clinical diagnosis rather than reliance on race or ethnicity alone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;Twin studies demonstrate the simultaneous onset of VKH in monozygotic twins, and additional twin and sibling analyses support a significant genetic contribution to disease susceptibility.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>VKH&#x000a0;syndrome typically presents in the 3rd or 4th decade of life, with a reported age range from 3 to 78 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r31">[31]</xref> Pediatric cases tend to be aggressive, longstanding, and difficult to treat, with a higher frequency of subretinal fibrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r32">[32]</xref> Older patients, generally over 65 years of age, exhibit an increased risk of optic disc hyperemia and choroidal detachments.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r33">[33]</xref> Female patients are affected more frequently than male patients, with reported female-to-male ratios ranging from 3:2 to 2:1.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The pathophysiology of VKH&#x000a0;syndrome reflects a complex autoimmune attack primarily targeting melanocyte-containing tissues in genetically susceptible individuals. Strong genetic predisposition is indicated by HLA associations, including HLA-DRB1*0405, HLA-DR4, and DR53 alleles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r34">[34]</xref></p>
        <p>Autoreactive CD4+ T-helper type 1 (Th1) and&#x000a0;T-helper type 17 (Th17) cells recognize antigens derived from melanocyte-related proteins, such as tyrosinase, tyrosinase-related protein 1, and&#x000a0;glycoprotein 100, in pigmented tissues.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r36">[36]</xref> Immune activation may be triggered or amplified by viral or environmental stimuli, resulting in loss of immune tolerance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r37">[37]</xref> Many patients report a prodromal flu-like illness preceding the onset of ocular, auditory, and cutaneous inflammation.</p>
        <p>Th1-mediated inflammation specifically targets melanocytes, with the tyrosinase peptide recognized by T cells, particularly in melanocytes expressing HLA-DRB1*0405.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r38">[38]</xref> Homology between the tyrosinase peptide and cytomegalovirus peptides has been demonstrated. Additional studies have detected Epstein&#x02013;Barr virus DNA in CSF of patients with VKH using polymerase chain reaction techniques.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>The immune response induces granulomatous inflammation of the choroidal stroma (primary stromal choroiditis), characterized by diffuse infiltration of choroidal melanocytes and disruption of the blood&#x02013;ocular barrier. Choroidal thickening and inflammation lead to multiple serous retinal detachments.</p>
        <p>Melanocyte-rich tissues in the meninges, inner ear, skin (vitiligo, poliosis), and hair are also targeted, accounting for the multisystem manifestations of VKH&#x000a0;syndrome. Persistent ocular inflammation, which may be subclinical, can progress to chronic depigmentation of the fundus (&#x0201c;sunset-glow fundus&#x0201d;) and structural damage to the retinal pigment epithelium (RPE) and choriocapillaris.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r40">[40]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Histopathological findings in VKH&#x000a0;syndrome&#x000a0;include nonnecrotizing, diffuse granulomatous inflammation, in contrast to the focal granulomatous inflammation observed in tuberculosis or sarcoidosis. The choroidal stroma of the uveal tract is primarily affected, with the choriocapillaris largely spared in the acute stage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r41">[41]</xref>&#x000a0;The infiltrate comprises lymphocytes, epithelioid histiocytes, and multinucleated giant cells, forming granulomas that produce Dalen&#x02013;Fuchs nodules between the RPE and the Bruch membrane. Hematoxylin and eosin staining of enucleated specimens demonstrates B and T cells within the choroid and loss of choroidal melanocytes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r42">[42]</xref></p>
        <p>Differentiation of VKH&#x000a0;syndrome from sympathetic ophthalmia is challenging histologically. Both conditions exhibit sparing of the choriocapillaris in the acute stage, nonnecrotizing diffuse granulomatous uveal inflammation in the acute phase, Dalen&#x02013;Fuchs nodules, chronic-phase melanocyte loss, and chorioretinal adhesion with scarring and choriocapillaris involvement in the chronic recurrent stage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r43">[43]</xref> The history of penetrating ocular injury remains a distinguishing feature unique to sympathetic ophthalmia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r44">[44]</xref></p>
        <p>Recurrent episodes of VKH&#x000a0;syndrome produce sunset-glow fundus, characterized by progressive fundus depigmentation in the setting of optic disc pallor, a marker of poor visual prognosis. Late-stage VKH&#x000a0;syndrome demonstrates peripheral fundus scars histopathologically composed of chorioretinal atrophy and RPE loss, rather than Dalen&#x02013;Fuchs nodules as previously thought.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r45">[45]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r47">[47]</xref> Dalen&#x02013;Fuchs nodules are typically confined to the posterior pole and equator and are observed during the acute phase.</p>
        <p>Histopathologic features vary across disease stages. In the acute stage, diffuse lymphocytic infiltration, primarily T cells with a T-helper/T-suppressor imbalance, and macrophage infiltration affect the uvea, including choroid, ciliary body, and iris. Focal aggregates of epithelioid cells and multinucleated giant cells containing pigment are present, without choroidal necrosis, and serous retinal detachments are common. During the convalescent stage, gradual loss of melanin granules from choroidal melanocytes produces a pale, depigmented fundus, and inflammation is predominantly nongranulomatous. Chronic recurrent disease demonstrates chorioretinal atrophy, scarring, focal hyperpigmentation, choriocapillaris involvement, and granulomatous inflammation.</p>
        <p>Extraocular tissues, including meninges, skin, and cochlea, may exhibit granulomatous infiltrates targeting melanocytes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r48">[48]</xref> Chronic inflammation ultimately resolves, leaving fibrosis and depigmentation as residual histologic hallmarks.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Patients with VKH typically present with a combination of systemic, neurological, and ocular manifestations that evolve through distinct clinical stages. The disease primarily affects young to middle-aged individuals, most frequently in the 3rd to 5th decade of life, with a female predominance and higher incidence among Asian, Middle Eastern, Hispanic, and Native American populations. Detailed history taking and recognition of sequential clinical phases are essential for early diagnosis and effective management.</p>
        <p>The prodromal phase often precedes ocular involvement by several days and resembles a viral illness, with headache, fever, malaise, nausea, neck stiffness, and photophobia. These symptoms reflect aseptic meningitis, an immune-mediated inflammation of melanocyte-containing meninges.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r49">[49]</xref> Cochlear involvement is suggested by tinnitus, vertigo, or hearing loss.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r50">[50]</xref> Absence of prior ocular trauma or surgery provides an important diagnostic clue, distinguishing VKH syndrome from sympathetic ophthalmia.</p>
        <p>Following the systemic prodrome, the ocular phase typically begins abruptly, presenting with bilateral blurred vision, photophobia, and ocular discomfort. Although both eyes are usually involved, symptoms may initially manifest asymmetrically. Central vision distortion or metamorphopsia may occur due to subretinal fluid accumulation from choroidal inflammation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r51">[51]</xref></p>
        <p>VKH progresses through 4 clinical phases: prodromal, acute uveitic, chronic uveitic (convalescent), and recurrent chronic. The prodromal phase is often self-limited, lasting hours to weeks, and exhibits subtle chorioretinal findings that may be overlooked without indocyanine green angiography (ICGA) and correlation with CSF analysis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r52">[52]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r53">[53]</xref> Systemic and neurological symptoms may mimic viral meningitis, including headache, neck stiffness, fever, auditory disturbances, dizziness, eye pain, and photophobia. History frequently reveals a recent recovery from a viral illness. Examination may demonstrate mild meningeal signs, and CSF analysis typically shows lymphocytic pleocytosis, confirming central nervous system involvement.</p>
        <p>The acute uveitic phase presents with blurred vision and photophobia. Bilateral granulomatous panuveitis develops within days to weeks. Anterior segment findings include cells and flare in the anterior chamber, mutton-fat keratic precipitates, posterior synechiae, and vitritis. Slit-lamp examination typically reveals vitreous cells.</p>
        <p>Posterior segment involvement demonstrates multiple serous (exudative) retinal detachments, optic disc hyperemia, and choroidal thickening. Optical coherence tomography (OCT) and fundus fluorescein angiography (FFA) reveal pinpoint leakage and pooling of dye. Focal subretinal fluid can coalesce or increase in height without prompt treatment&#x000a0;(see&#x000a0;<bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Subretinal Fluid in Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada Syndrome). The optic disc is frequently swollen and hyperemic, occasionally accompanied by hemorrhages or a macular fan or star simulating neuroretinitis&#x000a0;(see&#x000a0;<bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Optic Disc Edema in Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada Syndrome).</p>
        <p>Broad chorioretinal folds may occur due to choroidal thickening. Multifocal gray-white placoid lesions at the RPE, resembling but less well-defined than acute posterior multifocal placoid pigment epitheliopathy (APMPPE), may also be observed&#x000a0;(see&#x000a0;<bold>Image. </bold>Acute Fundus Findings in Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada Syndrome). Visual acuity is often markedly reduced during this stage.</p>
        <p>Edema secondary to inflammation can extend to the retina, optic nerve, or ciliary body, producing exudative retinal detachment, optic disc edema, and acute angle-closure glaucoma, respectively.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r54">[54]</xref> Inflammatory infiltration and edema of the ciliary body and anterior choroid may induce anterior rotation of the ciliary body, causing a shallow anterior chamber and angle-closure glaucoma, which responds to control of ocular inflammation.</p>
        <p>Inferior bullous exudative retinal detachments&#x000a0;typically exhibit shifting fluid with changes in body position from sitting to supine (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Inferior Exudative Retinal Detachment in Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada Syndrome). Chorioretinal folds, including radial or circumferential folds surrounding the optic disc (Paton lines), may be observed. Small, round nodules beneath the RPE (Dalen&#x02013;Fuchs nodules) are more clearly visualized using multicolor imaging at and around the posterior pole.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r55">[55]</xref> A 2025 study of 11 patients with serous retinal detachments demonstrated persistent color vision deficits following resolution of subretinal fluid.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r56">[56]</xref></p>
        <p>The chronic or convalescent phase develops following control of acute inflammation. Recovery is characterized by depigmentation of the uveal tract. Patchy loss of pigment from the RPE and choroid may be observed after resolution of subretinal fluid. Radial, irregular pigmented demarcation lines may radiate anteriorly from the optic disc.</p>
        <p>Multifocal, small, scalloped atrophic chorioretinal scars, resembling presumed ocular histoplasmosis syndrome (POHS), may appear, particularly in the inferior fundus, often in linear distributions. Histopathologic studies confirm that these lesions represent atrophy rather than Dalen&#x02013;Fuchs nodules. The hallmark ocular feature of this phase is the sunset-glow fundus, a red depigmented appearance resulting from diffuse choroidal and RPE depigmentation. The Sugiura sign, a perilimbal vitiligo ring, is characteristic.</p>
        <p>Systemic depigmentary changes, including vitiligo, poliosis, and alopecia, typically develop weeks to months after the acute phase. Additional extraocular manifestations include auditory abnormalities such as tinnitus, dysacusis, and hearing loss.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r57">[57]</xref></p>
        <p>The recurrent chronic phase is characterized by recurrent granulomatous anterior uveitis or chronic low-grade inflammation. This phase is frequently associated with complications, including cataract, glaucoma, choroidal neovascularization, iris neovascularization, optic disc neovascularization, and subretinal fibrosis. Inadequate or delayed immunosuppressive therapy increases the risk of recurrence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r58">[58]</xref></p>
        <p>The International Committee on Nomenclature of VKH defines 3 classifications: complete, incomplete, and suspected. Complete VKH disease requires both neurologic and dermatologic manifestations in addition to ocular findings. Incomplete VKH disease presents with either dermatologic symptoms or neurologic manifestations. Suspected VKH disease presents with intraocular findings without accompanying dermatologic or neurologic signs. All VKH syndrome classifications require exclusion of other causes of intraocular findings, including prior ocular trauma, surgery, or infectious etiologies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r59">[59]</xref>&#x000a0;The table below summarizes the 2001 revised diagnostic criteria for complete&#x000a0;VKH disease.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-140076.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table.&#x000a0;2001 Revised Diagnostic Criteria for Complete&#x000a0;Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada Syndrome</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellspacing="2" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Criterion</bold></p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p><bold>Details</bold></p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Requirement</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>All 5 main criteria must be satisfied for a diagnosis of complete VKH disease.</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>1. Trauma/surgery exclusion</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>No history of penetrating ocular trauma or intraocular surgery prior to the onset of uveitis</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>2. Other ocular disease exclusion</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>No clinical or laboratory evidence suggesting alternative ocular conditions</p>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>3. Bilateral ocular involvement</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Either 3a or 3b must be met, depending on the stage at the time of examination</p>
<p>a. Early manifestations:</p>
<list list-type="order"><list-item><p>Evidence of diffuse choroiditis, &#x000b1; anterior uveitis, vitreous inflammation, or optic disc hyperemia, which may manifest as:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Focal subretinal fluid, or</p></list-item><list-item><p>Bullous serous retinal detachments</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>If fundus findings are equivocal, both must be present:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>FFA showing focal choroidal perfusion delay, pinpoint leakage, large placoid hyperfluorescent areas, pooling in subretinal fluid, and optic nerve staining</p></list-item><list-item><p>Diffuse choroidal thickening without posterior scleritis on ultrasonography</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
<p>b. Late manifestations:</p>
<list list-type="order"><list-item><p>History suggestive of prior early-stage findings, plus either both (2) and (3) below, or multiple signs from (3):</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ocular depigmentation (any):
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Sunset-glow fundus, or</p></list-item><list-item><p>Sugiura sign</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item><list-item><p>Additional ocular signs (any):
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Nummular chorioretinal scars</p></list-item><list-item><p>RPE clumping or migration</p></list-item><list-item><p>Recurrent or chronic anterior uveitis</p></list-item></list>
</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>4. Neurological/auditory findings</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>May have resolved by examination. Any&#x000a0;1 of the following is sufficient:</p>
<list list-type="order"><list-item><p>Meningismus (malaise, fever, headache, nausea, abdominal pain, neck/back stiffness, or combination; headache alone is insufficient)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Tinnitus</p></list-item><list-item><p>CSF pleocytosis</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>5. Integumentary findings</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Must occur after&#x000a0;central nervous system or ocular disease onset. Any&#x000a0;1 of the following&#x000a0;is sufficient:</p>
<list list-type="order"><list-item><p>Alopecia</p></list-item><list-item><p>Poliosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Vitiligo</p></list-item></list>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Incomplete VKH disease is diagnosed when criteria 1 through 3 below are present together with either criterion 4 or criterion 5:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p>No history of penetrating ocular trauma or intraocular surgery preceding the initial onset of uveitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>No clinical or laboratory evidence suggestive of alternative ocular disease entities</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Bilateral ocular involvement</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neurologic/auditory findings, as defined for complete Vogt&#x02013;Koyanagi&#x02013;Harada disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Integumentary findings, as defined for complete Vogt&#x02013;Koyanagi&#x02013;Harada disease</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Probable Vogt&#x02013;Koyanagi&#x02013;Harada disease is diagnosed in cases of isolated ocular involvement, when criteria 1 through 3 below are satisfied:</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p>No history of penetrating ocular trauma or intraocular surgery preceding the initial onset of uveitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>No clinical or laboratory evidence suggestive of alternative ocular disease entities</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Bilateral ocular involvement, as defined for complete VKH disease</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>A typical VKH presentation begins with a preceding systemic illness, followed by bilateral visual disturbance and progressive depigmentation of ocular and cutaneous tissues. The disorder represents autoimmune-mediated destruction of melanocytes affecting ocular, meningeal, auditory, and integumentary systems.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>VKH syndrome is a clinical diagnosis based on a characteristic constellation of findings, supported by laboratory and imaging studies. A comprehensive history should assess prior episodes, meningeal symptoms, ocular or orbital pain, and auditory complaints. Physical examination and slit-lamp evaluation may demonstrate signs of previous inflammatory episodes. Poliosis or vitiligo typically indicates progression to the chronic uveitic phase of disease.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Ocular Investigations</bold>
</p>
        <p>OCT is a noninvasive imaging modality used to evaluate VKH and assist in diagnosis (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Optical Coherence Tomography Findings in Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada Syndrome). ICGA may provide superior sensitivity for monitoring chronic disease and its progression. In acute VKH, OCT can demonstrate intraretinal septated fluid (bacillary layer detachment), subretinal fluid, RPE undulations or corrugations, and increased choroidal volume with diffuse thickening, indicative of choroidal inflammation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r60">[60]</xref> OCT constitutes a key component of multimodal imaging to establish VKH diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r61">[61]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r62">[62]</xref></p>
        <p>Enhanced depth imaging (EDI) or swept-source OCT enables reliable quantification of choroidal thickness and volume, which increase in the acute phase and gradually normalize with systemic corticosteroid and immunosuppressive therapy. In chronic VKH, OCT frequently reveals choroidal thinning, RPE irregularity, outer retinal disruption, and subretinal fibrosis, all associated with poorer visual outcomes and persistent inflammation. Recurrent VKH may present as renewed choroidal thickening or increased choroidal volume on EDI-OCT despite a quiet slit-lamp examination, highlighting OCT&#x02019;s role as an objective biomarker of subclinical activity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r63">[63]</xref></p>
        <p>OCT angiography (OCTA) is a dyeless, noninvasive imaging modality under active investigation for VKH diagnosis and follow-up. In acute VKH, OCTA commonly demonstrates discrete flow voids in the choriocapillaris slab, representing areas of reduced perfusion that correspond to hypofluorescent lesions on ICGA and hyporeflective spots on EDI-OCT. Studies indicate that both the number and area of these flow voids decrease following initiation of high-dose systemic corticosteroids and immunosuppressive therapy, paralleling clinical improvement and ICGA normalization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r64">[64]</xref></p>
        <p>Widefield swept-source OCTA reveals reduced superficial and deep retinal capillary density in acute VKH, with partial recovery over time, suggesting microvascular alterations may extend beyond the choriocapillaris and potentially affect retinal function. Despite these advantages, OCTA remains complementary to ICGA due to limitations in the field of view, motion sensitivity, segmentation artifacts, and the lack of standardized quantitative metrics.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r65">[65]</xref></p>
        <p>Fundus autofluorescence (FAF) imaging demonstrates variable patterns in VKH disease, aiding noninvasive assessment of RPE changes across disease stages. In acute VKH with serous retinal detachment, short-wavelength (SW-AF) and near-infrared (NIR-AF) FAF show hypoautofluorescent signals due to blockage by subretinal fluid. A background of mild, uniform hyperautofluorescence appears in patients imaged soon after symptom onset, while diffuse, mottled hyperautofluorescence develops in patients imaged weeks later.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r66">[66]</xref>&#x000a0;Postresolution, numerous hypoautofluorescent granular dots appear in previously detached areas, indicating RPE damage or atrophy and correlating with FFA window defects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r67">[67]</xref></p>
        <p>Chronic VKH exhibits both hypoautofluorescence and hyperautofluorescence.&#x000a0;Hypoautofluorescence links to peripapillary atrophy, scars, or RPE loss, while hyperautofluorescence associates with cystoid macular edema or RPE proliferation.&#x000a0;Sunset-glow fundus, from choroidal depigmentation, shows no significant FAF change, distinguishing&#x000a0;this finding from true RPE atrophy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r68">[68]</xref> Ultra-widefield FAF highlights peripheral abnormalities in chronic cases, including multifocal hypoautofluorescent spots, hyperautofluorescent spots, and lattice-like patterns.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r69">[69]</xref></p>
        <p>B-scan ultrasonography remains valuable in VKH, particularly when media opacities, such as dense vitritis or cataract, limit fundus visualization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r70">[70]</xref> In acute VKH, B-scan typically shows diffuse, low- to medium-reflective choroidal thickening,&#x000a0;with posterior predominance and bilateral involvement, indicating intense choroidal stromal inflammation (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;B-Scan Ultrasonography Findings in Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada Syndrome).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r71">[71]</xref> Smooth, dome-shaped serous retinal detachments with acoustically clear subretinal fluid are frequently observed and correlate with sudden visual decline. Serial scans during treatment demonstrate gradual normalization of choroidal thickness and retinal reattachment, providing objective confirmation of therapeutic response.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r72">[72]</xref></p>
        <p>Ultrasound biomicroscopy (UBM) in VKH shows a thickened, swollen ciliary body with low internal reflectivity and obscured ciliary processes, reflecting intense uveal inflammation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r73">[73]</xref> UBM can also detect ciliochoroidal detachment, supraciliary fluid, a shallow anterior chamber, and anterior rotation of the ciliary body&#x02013;iris complex, which explains transient angle-closure and intraocular pressure (IOP) spikes observed early in the disease course.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r74">[74]</xref></p>
        <p>FFA in acute VKH typically demonstrates multiple pinpoint hyperfluorescent leaks, with fluorescence that increases in size and intensity over time, producing a &#x0201c;starry sky&#x0201d; pattern (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Pinpoint Leakage on Fluorescein Angiography). Leaks occur at the level of the RPE in the early phase, with pooling of dye in subretinal spaces during the late phase (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Late-Phase Fluorescein Angiography in Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada Syndrome). Early-phase FFA may also&#x000a0;show focal or patchy areas of choroidal hypofluorescence, indicating delayed choroidal filling (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Early-Phase Fluorescein Angiography in Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada Syndrome).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r75">[75]</xref> Optic disc leakage and serous detachment of the neurosensory retina are frequently observed. Placoid subretinal areas of dye pooling may occur in some patients in the late phase.</p>
        <p>Retinal paravascular staining suggestive of retinal vasculitis may be noted. Alternate bands of hyperfluorescence and hypofluorescence indicate choroidal folds.</p>
        <p>In the chronic stage, FFA patterns include spotted hyperfluorescence and hypofluorescence (&#x0201c;salt-and-pepper&#x0201d;), hyperfluorescent window defects, and a &#x0201c;moth-eaten&#x0201d; appearance. Convalescent-stage FFA may also demonstrate spotted hyperfluorescence and hypofluorescence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r76">[76]</xref></p>
        <p>ICGA may be used for diagnostic purposes and is highly valuable for monitoring disease activity and treatment response.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r77">[77]</xref> ICGA directly evaluates the choroid and RPE, the 2 structures most affected in the early course of VKH. The wavelengths used in ICGA penetrate the choroid effectively with reduced interference from the RPE compared to FFA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r78">[78]</xref> Studies report 90% to 100% sensitivity for ICGA in diagnosing VKH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r79">[79]</xref> ICGA is considered the gold standard for detecting choroidal stromal inflammation.</p>
        <p>Acute-stage ICGA typically demonstrates multiple hypofluorescent dark dots representing choroidal granulomas, along with diffuse choroidal hyperfluorescence that often persists longer than FFA abnormalities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r80">[80]</xref> Key ICGA findings include early choroidal stromal vessel hyperfluorescence and leakage;&#x000a0;hypofluorescent dark dots;&#x000a0;fuzzy vascular pattern of large stromal vessels in the intermediate phase; and disc hyperfluorescence. Hypofluorescent dark dots are the most consistent and prominent sign, resolving 90%&#x000a0;to 100% after 4 months of therapy. Other findings, including early stromal vessel hyperfluorescence, fuzzy intermediate-phase vessels, and disc hyperfluorescence, resolve within 2 months of treatment.</p>
        <p>Treatment consisted of pulse intravenous methylprednisolone (IVMP) 500&#x000a0;to 1,000 mg for 3 days, followed by high-dose oral corticosteroids (0.8&#x02013;1.5 mg/kg) tapered slowly. Immunosuppressive agents were added for insufficient response. ICGA is particularly useful for detecting subclinical choroidal activity during immunosuppression tapering, as hypofluorescent dark spots may appear even when clinical signs and FFA&#x000a0;findings are unremarkable.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r81">[81]</xref></p>
        <p>Multifocal and full-field electroretinography may&#x000a0;demonstrate reduced amplitudes, indicating diffuse photoreceptor dysfunction in eyes with recurrent or chronic VKH, even when best-corrected visual acuity remains relatively preserved. Visual field testing frequently demonstrates central or paracentral scotomas and enlarged blind spots during the acute stage, which can progress to more complex defects in chronic disease, reflecting structural macular and optic nerve changes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Systemic Workup&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>Routine laboratory investigations, including complete blood count, liver and renal function tests, and inflammatory markers such as erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein, are generally nonspecific in VKH. These tests are valuable for excluding infectious etiologies and monitoring treatment safety during systemic corticosteroid or immunosuppressant administration. Targeted serologic tests for syphilis, tuberculosis, sarcoidosis, and other infectious or inflammatory mimickers are essential before confirming VKH, given the overlapping phenotype with other causes of granulomatous uveitis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r82">[82]</xref></p>
        <p>Although no pathognomonic serum biomarker exists, elevated serum immunoglobulin E (IgE) has been associated with more severe vascular leakage, greater macular edema, and poorer visual outcomes in acute VKH, suggesting a potential role as a marker of inflammatory burden. Additional studies have explored correlations between serum immunoglobulin profiles and choroidal or retinal imaging findings, but such markers remain investigational and are not yet incorporated into routine clinical decision-making.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r83">[83]</xref></p>
        <p>CSF analysis in the prodromal or early acute phase frequently shows lymphocytic pleocytosis with elevated protein and normal glucose, consistent with aseptic meningitis and reflecting inflammation of melanocyte-rich meninges. CSF abnormalities appear in a high proportion of early VKH cases and typically resolve with systemic corticosteroid therapy. Lumbar puncture is most informative when performed near symptom onset in patients with&#x000a0;neurological manifestations, such as headache or neck stiffness.</p>
        <p>Neuroimaging with magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) can demonstrate pachymeningeal or leptomeningeal enhancement, thickening and enhancement of the optic nerve sheath, bilateral choroidal enhancement with scleral sparing, retinal detachment, and periventricular white matter lesions on FLAIR (fluid-attenuated inversion recovery) and T2, supporting a diffuse inflammatory process involving melanocyte-containing structures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r84">[84]</xref> MRI also helps exclude infectious meningitis, demyelinating disease, or neoplastic processes, increasing diagnostic confidence when combined with ocular and CSF findings.</p>
        <p>Audiological evaluation often reveals bilateral, predominantly high-frequency sensorineural hearing loss (SNHL), even when patients report only subtle tinnitus or aural fullness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r85">[85]</xref> These findings reflect autoimmune inflammation targeting the cochlea and vestibular apparatus and may fluctuate with systemic disease activity and immunosuppressive therapy.</p>
        <p>Dermatologic and hair examination often reveals vitiligo, poliosis, and nonscarring alopecia during the convalescent and chronic phases, providing visible evidence of systemic melanocyte loss.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r86">[86]</xref> Recognition of these integumentary signs aids diagnosis and supports holistic care, given the potential psychosocial impact despite quiescent intraocular inflammation.</p>
        <p>VKH demonstrates a strong genetic association with HLA-DR4 alleles, particularly HLA-DRB1*0405, across multiple ethnic populations, supporting a major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class II&#x02013;restricted autoimmune response against melanocyte antigens. These alleles likely facilitate presentation of melanocyte-derived peptides, such as tyrosinase, to autoreactive T cells, lowering the disease threshold in susceptible individuals.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r87">[87]</xref> Immunologic profiling reveals a predominant Th1 and Th17 signature, with elevated interferon-&#x003b3; and IL-17 levels in blood and intraocular fluid during active disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r88">[88]</xref> IL-17F (rs763780) polymorphisms correlate with increased susceptibility in Chinese Han patients, indicating a contributory role of IL-17&#x02013;mediated immunity in pathogenesis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r89">[89]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Acute VKH was historically managed primarily with high-dose systemic corticosteroids, typically beginning with IVMP 1,000 mg daily for 3 consecutive days, followed by oral prednisolone 1&#x000a0;to 2 mg/kg/day with gradual tapering over a minimum of 6 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r90">[90]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r91">[91]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r92">[92]</xref> Extended systemic corticosteroid therapy (&#x02265;6 months) has been associated with lower relapse rates and improved visual outcomes compared with shorter courses.&#x000a0;A study demonstrated that early withdrawal of oral corticosteroids increased the risk of recurrence more than 8 times.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r93">[93]</xref></p>
        <p>Recent cohorts indicate that early introduction of immunomodulatory therapy (IMT) in combination with corticosteroids significantly reduces relapse, improves final visual acuity, and decreases the risk of sunset-glow fundus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r94">[94]</xref> Color vision and S-cone function, often profoundly impaired during the acute exudative phase, recover substantially within 12 months when inflammation and serous retinal detachment are rapidly and aggressively managed.</p>
        <p>Posterior sub-Tenon (PST) injection of triamcinolone may be considered in addition to systemic therapy, particularly in patients who cannot tolerate systemic therapy or have contraindications. PST alone has achieved complete resolution without recurrence in approximately 80% of patients with acute VKH without systemic disorders.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r95">[95]</xref> Use of local therapy alone in a systemic disease such as VKH remains controversial and is not routinely practiced. Case reports support PST injection as a steroid-sparing option in special circumstances, including pregnancy, where bilateral PST injections produced rapid anatomic recovery without recurrence for more than 1 year.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r96">[96]</xref> Risks of elevated IOP and cataract formation, as well as alternative therapeutic options, require discussion prior to periocular or intraocular steroid administration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r97">[97]</xref></p>
        <p>Fluocinolone intravitreal implant reduces the recurrence rate of ocular inflammation in implanted eyes compared with nonimplanted eyes over 3 years.&#x000a0;In the study by Heo et al, approximately 70% of implanted eyes developed high IOP, and all phakic eyes developed cataracts.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r98">[98]</xref> Combined with intravitreal methotrexate (MTX), fluocinolone implants may achieve long-term remission in refractory VKH cases with severe pain and macular edema.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r99">[99]</xref></p>
        <p>Intravitreal dexamethasone implant (700 mcg) improves visual acuity, reduces systemic steroid burden, and decreases macular edema in patients with VKH who have received systemic steroids and immunosuppressive agents.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r100">[100]</xref> The implant is effective in cases with recurrent macular edema despite multiple IVMP&#x000a0;courses, oral steroids, and immunosuppressants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r101">[101]</xref> Successful use has been reported at or after 2 weeks from diagnosis in patients with VKH treated with IVMP followed by oral prednisolone, cyclosporine A (CsA), and immunosuppressants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r102">[102]</xref></p>
        <p>Intravitreal dexamethasone implant also provides an option for patients who develop systemic side effects from immunosuppressants, including hepatic dysfunction from azathioprine or adalimumab, and may result in recurrence-free follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r103">[103]</xref> VKH remains a systemic disease, and local therapy does not address integumentary or auditory manifestations. Local steroid therapy, including peribulbar, PST, or intravitreal injections, may be considered for patients who are already receiving systemic IMT,&#x000a0;have minimal systemic symptoms, or cannot tolerate systemic therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r104">[104]</xref></p>
        <p>IMT has transformed VKH management, shifting from prolonged high-dose corticosteroids to steroid-sparing regimens that improve visual outcomes and reduce complications. VKH represents one of the few absolute indications for early IMT initiation. Early introduction of IMT alongside steroids significantly lowers relapse rates and prevents chronic sequelae, such as sunset-glow fundus, compared with steroid monotherapy.</p>
        <p>Conventional IMT agents, including MTX, mycophenolate mofetil (MMF), azathioprine, CsA, and tacrolimus, control inflammation in 60% to 90% of patients when combined with tapering steroids. MTX (7.5&#x02013;25 mg/week) induces remission in approximately 50% of cases but may have high relapse rates, up to 50%, and requires 3 to 6 months for peak effect. MMF (1&#x02013;3 g/day), the most commonly used 1st-line IMT agent, controlled acute VKH in 50% of cases in&#x000a0;a series but required augmentation in 1/2 of those cases. CsA (2&#x02013;5 mg/kg/day) is effective in chronic VKH but carries a high discontinuation rate, sometimes exceeding 60%, due to renal toxicity and hypertension.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r105">[105]</xref></p>
        <p>Biologics have emerged as a treatment option for refractory VKH, with agents suppressing tumor necrosis factor at the forefront.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r106">[106]</xref> Adalimumab achieved quiescence in 70%&#x000a0;to 80% of steroid-dependent cases, often permitting steroid withdrawal within 6 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r107">[107]</xref> A randomized trial demonstrated that CsA plus steroids is noninferior to adalimumab plus steroids for 26-week visual improvement in late-phase VKH, with both regimens outperforming historical steroid-alone controls.</p>
        <p>Infliximab combined with MTX successfully controlled inflammation in a pediatric patient with VKH syndrome, with sustained remission reported over a 10-year follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r108">[108]</xref> A survey of American Uveitis Society members indicated that, although MTX and MMF were commonly used as 1st-line therapy, infliximab and adalimumab were perceived as the most effective treatments for VKH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r109">[109]</xref></p>
        <p>IMT selection depends on disease phase, patient age, comorbidities, and pregnancy status. MMF or MTX may be preferred as 1st-line IMT for acute adult VKH cases. Pediatric VKH may be managed with MTX and adalimumab or infliximab.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r110">[110]</xref> Recurrent, refractory, steroid-intolerant, or chronic VKH may require adalimumab,&#x000a0;CsA, or both.</p>
        <p>Treatment options during the 2nd and 3rd trimesters of pregnancy include systemic steroids, adalimumab, and azathioprine. Risks, benefits, and alternatives of therapy in pregnancy require detailed discussion with the patient and family. Interprofessional collaboration, including consultation with an obstetrician, is essential prior to initiating such therapy.</p>
        <p>IMT carries potential adverse effects, including hepatotoxicity (MTX), gastrointestinal upset (MMF), nephrotoxicity (CsA), teratogenicity, and infusion reactions (biologics), necessitating careful monitoring. Recent data support IMT as 1st-line adjunct therapy within 2 to 3 weeks of onset to optimize outcomes and minimize depigmentation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r111">[111]</xref> Ongoing trials and registries aim to refine optimal sequencing and duration of therapy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Clinical features resembling VKH occur in multiple disorders. The most common differential diagnoses&#x000a0;are explained below.</p>
        <p>Sympathetic ophthalmia presents with bilateral serous retinal detachments, papillitis, multifocal pinpoint leaks on FFA, and hypofluorescent dark spots on ICGA, which may be clinically indistinguishable from VKH if a history of penetrating ocular trauma is unknown.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r112">[112]</xref> Sympathetic ophthalmia is typically distinguished by a history of ocular penetrating trauma or surgery, which is a hallmark feature and is excluded by VKH diagnostic criteria. The uveitis pattern in&#x000a0;sympathetic ophthalmia is often unpredictable, unlike VKH&#x02019;s staged progression, and systemic features are uncommon.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r113">[113]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r114">[114]</xref></p>
        <p>Central serous chorioretinopathy (CSCR) can present with serous retinal detachments, particularly following systemic corticosteroid use. Features of intraocular inflammation are absent in CSCR, and the condition is usually asymmetric. OCT demonstrates serous pigment epithelial detachments with a straight RPE, in contrast to the RPE undulations observed in VKH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r115">[115]</xref> FFA in CSCR shows characteristic inkblot, smoke-stack, or diffuse leaks without optic disc hyperfluorescence, whereas VKH exhibits multiple pinpoint leaks with a hot optic disc.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r116">[116]</xref> CSCR typically resolves after cessation of steroids, while VKH requires systemic corticosteroid therapy for management.</p>
        <p>Acute APMPPE and VKH share several clinical features. Both conditions can present with a preceding viral or flu-like prodrome, headache, and rapid visual loss, accompanied by yellowish-white placoid lesions in the fundus. Subretinal fluid, occasionally with septa on OCT, may be observed in both conditions. Choroidal thickening and hypofluorescent lesions on ICGA are also common to both.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r117">[117]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r118">[118]</xref> Some authors have proposed that APMPPE and VKH may represent a spectrum of related disease processes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r119">[119]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r120">[120]</xref></p>
        <p>APMPPE is typically self-resolving, with placoid creamy lesions and minimal or absent vitreous inflammation. Systemic symptoms are uncommon, aside from occasional manifestations related to cerebral vasculitis. FFA demonstrates early hypofluorescent placoid areas with late staining. Results of CSF analysis are usually unremarkable, and visual prognosis is favorable even without treatment.</p>
        <p>Leukemic infiltration can produce uveitis, choroiditis, or exudative retinal detachment, occasionally demonstrating pinpoint leaks on FFA similar to those observed in VKH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r121">[121]</xref> This phenomenon has been reported in both acute and chronic leukemia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r122">[122]</xref>&#x000a0;Systemic hematologic signs, including anemia or leukocytosis, along with confirmation by blood or bone marrow examination, distinguish leukemia from VKH.</p>
        <p>Uveal lymphoid infiltration, including reactive or immunoglobulin G4&#x02013;related disease, can present with choroidal thickening and uveitis, masquerading as VKH. These cases often occur in older patients, are unilateral or asymmetric, and may lack the systemic features characteristic of VKH. Biopsy assists in definitive diagnosis.</p>
        <p>Intraocular lymphoma can mimic chronic VKH through vitritis and subretinal infiltrates.&#x000a0;This malignancy&#x000a0;typically affects older patients, progresses insidiously, responds poorly to corticosteroids, and is confirmed by cytology or biopsy.</p>
        <p>Ocular Lyme disease may produce bilateral uveitis, choroiditis, and optic disc swelling. A history of tick exposure and a positive Lyme serology result help differentiate this condition from VKH, which exhibits characteristic integumentary and neurologic findings.</p>
        <p>Sarcoidosis can mimic VKH through granulomatous uveitis and choroiditis. Distinguishing features include systemic involvement, such as pulmonary or lymph node disease, presence of noncaseating granulomas, elevated angiotensin-converting enzyme levels, and supportive imaging. Granulomatous involvement in sarcoidosis is typically focal, whereas VKH demonstrates diffuse inflammation.</p>
        <p>Uveal effusion syndrome may produce serous or exudative retinal detachments similar to VKH. Absence of granulomatous inflammation or uveitis, presence of nanophthalmos or thickened sclera, and lack of systemic VKH signs differentiate this condition.</p>
        <p>Lupus choroidopathy results from autoimmune vascular changes, causing exudative detachments in patients with systemic lupus erythematosus. Clinical differentiation relies on systemic manifestations, laboratory findings (including renal and serologic tests), and features resembling Purtscher-like retinopathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r123">[123]</xref></p>
        <p>Posterior scleritis can present with ocular pain, retinal or serous detachments, and choroidal thickening. The condition is often painful, demonstrates a &#x0201c;T-sign&#x0201d; on B-scan ultrasonography, and lacks VKH-associated integumentary or neurologic features.</p>
        <p>Cat scratch disease caused by <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> species may mimic uveitis with neuroretinitis or serous detachments. Diagnosis relies on a history of cat exposure and a positive <italic toggle="yes">Bartonella</italic> serology result, with posterior segment findings differing from VKH.</p>
        <p>Metastatic ocular carcinoma can produce subretinal fluid or detachments.&#x000a0;Lesions typically present focally and unilaterally and are associated with a known systemic malignancy. Diagnosis is confirmed by biopsy.</p>
        <p>Exudative detachments secondary to malignant hypertension can resemble VKH with bilateral choroidopathy and serous detachments.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r124">[124]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r125">[125]</xref> Distinguishing features include markedly elevated blood pressure, systemic vascular signs, and absence of granulomatous uveitis or VKH integumentary and neurologic manifestations.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s11" sec-type="Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials">
        <title>Pertinent Studies and Ongoing Trials</title>
        <p>Significant interest exists in developing disease-specific therapies to reduce medication toxicity and minimize undesirable side effects. MicroRNA research has identified a potential disease-specific mechanism and is under investigation for clinical application in VKH. Interferon-&#x003b3; and IL-6 have been recognized as potential targets for future therapeutic interventions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r126">[126]</xref> Small studies have demonstrated disease control using JAK (Janus kinase) inhibition and IL-17/IL-23 blockade.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r127">[127]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r128">[128]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s12" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>VKH may have a relatively favorable prognosis if treated early and effectively. Prognosis worsens cumulatively with recurrent disease episodes. Poor prognostic indicators include age at disease onset and the frequency of recurrences. Sunset-glow fundus, a marker of the most severe stage of VKH and an indicator of poor visual function, can develop even when earlier-stage clinical findings are controlled.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r129">[129]</xref> Comparative studies demonstrate that combination therapy with corticosteroids and supplementary IMT significantly reduces sunset-glow fundus rates from 60% to 16%. Rates of subretinal fibrosis, an untreatable complication that can cause permanent vision loss, also decrease from 34% to 16% in patients treated early with combination therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r130">[130]</xref></p>
        <p>A study of 68 patients with acute VKH evaluated prognostic factors for visual outcomes, complications, and recurrent inflammation. Good initial visual acuity (better than 20/200), prolonged corticosteroid therapy (&#x0003e;9 months) with slow tapering, absence of posterior synechiae, and lack of complications or extraocular manifestations predicted final vision of 20/20. Older age, poor initial vision, and posterior synechiae increased complication risk. Rapid corticosteroid tapering, severe anterior chamber inflammation, poor initial vision, and extraocular manifestations were associated with recurrent inflammation. Careful corticosteroid management and early favorable clinical findings are critical for preserving vision.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r131">[131]</xref></p>
        <p>A separate study of 69 pediatric patients (age &#x0003c; 18 years) found that chronic recurrent VKH correlated with earlier onset, severe anterior chamber reaction, and corneal involvement. Complications included cataract (23.9%), glaucoma (39.1%), and choroidal neovascular membranes (26.1%), with higher rates in recurrent cases. Visual acuity improved from 20/70 to 20/50 overall. Despite complications, most pediatric patients achieved favorable long-term visual outcomes, although recurrent VKH was associated with worse results.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r132">[132]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s13" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>VKH is a systemic disease, and complications may include hearing loss, dysacusis, tinnitus, aural fullness, vertigo, vitiligo, and ophthalmic manifestations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r133">[133]</xref> SNHL occurs in up to 68% of patients. Most cases of SNHL in VKH resolve with appropriate corticosteroid therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r134">[134]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r135">[135]</xref> IMT has demonstrated efficacy in controlling auditory symptoms. However, some reports indicate limited benefit in patients unable to tolerate corticosteroids.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r136">[136]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r137">[137]</xref> Hearing loss may improve with IMT, although cochlear implants remain necessary in some cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r138">[138]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r139">[139]</xref> Intratympanic corticosteroid injection has shown benefit in patients who cannot tolerate systemic corticosteroids.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r140">[140]</xref>&#x000a0;Vertigo is less common, affecting 17%&#x000a0;to 19% of patients, but it&#x000a0;can cause substantial morbidity. This symptom responds favorably to corticosteroid therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r141">[141]</xref></p>
        <p>The most common integumentary manifestations of VKH are poliosis and vitiligo. Progression of poliosis and vitiligo slows and responds favorably to systemic corticosteroid therapy and IMT.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r142">[142]</xref> Topical calcineurin inhibitors and corticosteroids have demonstrated the ability to reverse skin depigmentation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r143">[143]</xref></p>
        <p>VKH can induce ciliary body inflammation and edema during the acute uveitic phase, resulting in anterior rotation of the ciliary body and closure of the anterior segment angle. This process may lead to secondary acute angle-closure glaucoma, a mechanism similar to that observed after topiramate use or panretinal photocoagulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r144">[144]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients may develop IOP elevation, with a median&#x000a0;value of 32.9 mm Hg (range 27&#x02013;40 mm Hg). Conventional IOP-lowering therapy is frequently ineffective. The mean age of affected patients is approximately 55.6 years, with a female predominance. Headache accompanied by sudden visual decline is a typical presenting feature. UBM is critical for diagnosing uveal effusion, ciliary body detachment, and anterior rotation of the ciliary body. Management of inflammation with corticosteroids remains the primary strategy for controlling IOP in these cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r145">[145]</xref></p>
        <p>Visually significant ophthalmic complications of chronic VKH include cataracts, glaucoma, neovascular glaucoma, macular holes, chronic retinal detachments, subretinal fibrosis, and choroidal neovascularization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r146">[146]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r147">[147]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r148">[148]</xref> Cataracts occur in up to 45% of patients and are frequently associated with intraocular inflammation and corticosteroid therapy, often resulting in posterior subcapsular cataracts.</p>
        <p>Cataract surgery generally yields favorable outcomes once inflammation has been controlled for 3 months, with significant visual improvement observed in 99% of eyes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r149">[149]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r150">[150]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r151">[151]</xref>&#x000a0;A study reported satisfactory postoperative results with only&#x000a0;1&#x000a0;month of inflammation control using a combination of IMT and corticosteroids. Postoperative complications are uncommon.&#x000a0;Approximately 10% of eyes develop hyphema, and 13% experience ocular hypertension, with both conditions typically resolving spontaneously. Consistent with an intraocular inflammatory disorder, posterior capsular opacification develops in up to 50% of patients following phacoemulsification.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r152">[152]</xref></p>
        <p>Ocular hypertension and glaucomatous optic neuropathy are common complications in patients with VKH. Glaucoma develops in approximately 6% to 45% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r153">[153]</xref> Significant risk factors for ocular hypertension include uveal effusion, poor visual acuity at initial presentation, delayed treatment initiation, posterior synechiae, and having more than 3 recurrent disease episodes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r154">[154]</xref> Long-term topical and systemic corticosteroid therapy also contributes substantially to glaucoma risk in VKH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r155">[155]</xref> The primary mechanism of glaucoma in VKH is open-angle rather than steroid-induced. However, a significant proportion of VKH patients develop glaucomatous optic neuropathy secondary to pupillary block caused by posterior synechiae.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r156">[156]</xref></p>
        <p>Subretinal fibrosis represents a significant complication of VKH, often resulting in severe visual decline.&#x000a0;Risk factors include pediatric onset, particularly in aggressive or long-standing disease; prolonged symptom duration before treatment; extensive subretinal fluid accumulation, indicative of serous or bullous detachment, at diagnosis; frequent recurrences; and marked ocular inflammation.&#x000a0;Subretinal fibrosis develops in up to 40% of eyes with VKH.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r157">[157]</xref></p>
        <p>The onset of subretinal fibrosis occurs earlier in Hispanic patients, approximately 6.5 months after disease onset, compared with 6.5 years in non-Hispanic patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r158">[158]</xref> Subretinal fibrosis constitutes a poor prognostic indicator, with multiple studies demonstrating progression despite combined corticosteroid and IMT therapy.&#x000a0;A study reported favorable outcomes with the combination of MMF and systemic corticosteroids.</p>
        <p>Subretinal fibrosis can precipitate macular detachments, severely compromising vision. Surgical management of these detachments may involve pars plana vitrectomy, excision of subretinal fibrosis, and vitreous tamponade.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r159">[159]</xref> Additional ocular complications reported in VKH include scleromalacia and choroidal osteoma.</p>
        <p>Choroidal neovascular membranes develop in 14% to 17% of patients with VKH. These lesions respond to vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) blockade therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r160">[160]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r161">[161]</xref></p>
        <p>Pediatric VKH remains a challenging disorder to manage, with a high risk of poor final visual acuity. Chronic recurrent cases present at an earlier age and demonstrate more severe anterior chamber inflammation, with pronounced corneal involvement, including band keratopathy. Complications in this population include corneal involvement, cataract in approximately 24% of cases, glaucoma in approximately 40%, and choroidal neovascular membranes in approximately 26% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r162">[162]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s14" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Appropriate referrals are essential for the management of integumentary and auditory manifestations in VKH. Timely consultation with audiology, otorhinolaryngology, and dermatology facilitates ongoing monitoring of disease progression.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s15" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patient education is critical to emphasize that VKH is a systemic disease with potential for relapse and recurrence. Untreated recurrent episodes result in progressively worse outcomes, often necessitating lifelong IMT.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r163">[163]</xref> Patients should be informed about the range of disease manifestations to enable early recognition of relapse. Ongoing management should involve coordinated care from multiple specialists, including neurologists, ophthalmologists, dermatologists, and otorhinolaryngologists.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s16" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Although rare, VKH should be considered in patients presenting with eye pain, associated headache, or meningeal signs. Therapeutic goals include controlling clinically apparent disease and preventing progression of VKH complications through early identification and treatment. Corticosteroids remain a cornerstone of therapy, but monotherapy without adjunctive IMT is associated with higher rates of recurrence and complications.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s17" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>When detected early, VKH syndrome may be effectively managed through coordinated care involving primary care physicians, emergency medicine practitioners, and specialists in low vision, otorhinolaryngology, ophthalmology, neurology, and audiology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r164">[164]</xref> Disease manifestations may be subtle, including symptoms that mimic aseptic meningitis, tinnitus, and vague visual disturbances, often accompanied by photophobia, floaters, or blurred vision.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r165">[165]</xref> Early intervention prevents significant morbidity, particularly from retinal complications. Most vision loss results from repeated cycles of acute inflammation and may occur in the absence of apparent symptoms. Patients are at risk of developing secondary complications without timely management,&#x000a0;including severe vision impairment or blindness.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140076.r166">[166]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140076.s18">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
